 was a tavern so there was this is  a place that my dad and then later when  he came to live with us my grandpa would  would go and have some beers and it was  kind of your typical 1970s Wisconsin  tavern not very well lit kind of grimy  smoke everywhere cuz everybody smoked  constantly including my dad and my  grandpa and he would sometimes stop  baloff after work he worked in Milwaukee  and had about an hour commute sometimes  he'd stop off and have a beer and then  come home and sometimes we would stop  there for other things and what I  remembered most about it before it  became a place where you could actually  have have supper and they had a few  things you could eat there mostly I  think like chips or maybe they could  make hamburgers or something was you  know my dad would come in and he would  sit down and there they'd pour a beer  and he'd sit there and [ __ ] chat with  the guys and he'd give me some quarters  to go play some games and one of the  things that was really interesting I  mean arcade games were becoming big in  the the late 70s and so you could play  some of those but they had this bowling  game and my sister and I used to love to  play it and it you know I should  research this I've never found it  anywhere else you had something like a  puck except it was made out of metal and  you pushed it down the the aisle which  was you know supposed to represent a  bowling alley and it was it was kind of  waxed and it would like run over things  and then these these pins would go up  and it was essentially an analogue  machine although I think there might  have been some digital components to it  like the scorekeeping mechanism again  kind of one-of-a-kind thing I've never  seen it anywhere else and we used to  love playing game and of course it  consumed quarters and quarters were  quite a bit of money back then so you  know you had to kind of nurse them you  didn't play them all  once you know I suppose you know rich  kids probably could but if you were we  were sort of solidly middle class then  when my dad was alive and and working  and and we were doing what we felt to be  quite well so Max's was a place where  you know I would see my dad hanging out  with these other guys who I didn't know  and they'd all be you know smoking  sitting around the bar chatting about  different things and he'd be there with  his you know white hair and his his you  know salt-and-pepper moustache looking  older than his years you know having a  beer and talking with them and then  eventually Max got real smart and he  realized that there weren't a lot of  restaurants around in the Wales area  other than you know the whistling toad  down to the South which is a supper club  which kind of pricey so he decided to  build an addition and in the addition he  put out a whole bunch of tables so it  became a restaurant and he started doing  Friday fish fries and so this was a big  thing for us you know now we could  actually go out more than once a month  because there would be specials so we  could save some money and the food was  really good I I remember it being quite  tasty and so by then my grandfather my  dad's dad was living with us and it  would be all five of us that is my mom  my dad my grandpa my sister and then me  all going out and getting a table and  we'd sit around and chitchat and do all  the sort of things that you'd have in a  place first of course the bread would  come and you know they tell you don't  want up on bread you know and we'd have  the option of getting kiddie cocktails  which would be like a Shirley Temple  which i think is just like basically  grenadine and soda  maybe with something else there's a like  a cherry added to it we could get  grasshoppers but they were not alcoholic  as far as I know you know and here's the  story that I really want to tell because  I think it's kind of a funny one so you  know we would go to this fish fry and he  had all these different options and that  was really cool too because you know I  think we kind of take this for granted  now you go into a restaurant  there's like a million different  possibilities just about anywhere that  you you might go and a lot of places  back then just didn't have that sort of  variety or what they had wasn't really  that good so max is it was basically  like stakes of different sorts I think  he also had like pork chops and chicken  and then because it was Friday you had  all sorts of fish stuff so you had like  you know your choice of different fish  you could get shrimp you could get  Lobster you could get combination plates  of different sorts and I remember I was  10 years old when my dad and my grandpa  made a bet now I was a kid who could eat  and you know looking at me now you might  say yeah well of course you were I was  super super skinny I was so skinny from  like you know my childhood into my early  teen years that I didn't like to wear  short sleeve shirts because they showed  how skinny my arms were as it turns out  my adopted dad my dad you know I'd  usually just call him he also was like  super skinny as a kid I was so skinny  that my ribs like stuck out and and I  could eat and eat and eat and eat and  they'd seen me eat my dad my grandpa you  know like my dad would make ribs and  they they talked about me eyeing up  stuff like I'd be eating and I'd already  be looking at the next thing that I was  gonna get like a you know cob of corn or  ribs or whatever it was gonna be right  and so my dad said to his dad  I bet Greg can eat a full sized steak  and a lobster tail because that was one  of the options it was like one of the  big entrees that you could get for Max's  place and my grandpa said no that kids  not gonna eat that much there's no way  that's gonna happen and they said this  after I'd already like loaded up on some  Brad and I think we had hors d'oeuvres  too another thing that they bring is  like pickles and olives and carrot  sticks and stuff like that and you know  being the kind of kid I was I eat  everything that was put in front of me I  didn't even eat the parsley off the  plate the non-functional garnish so my  dad said well what do you think are you  still hungry enough can you can you eat  this I think it was like a 12 ounce  New York strip steak and a big lobster  tail and these were not little lobster  tails like the the restaurants that can  are kind of cheap give you is this thing  was like you know big size right and I  was like yeah of course I can and I  never had lobster before that too so I  was kind of taking a chance but I was  the kind of kid who almost like eating  everything anyway the only thing that I  didn't like that we would have sometimes  was liver and that's a whole different  culinary conversation so you know they  you know they shake hands and they bet  ten bucks that's a lot of money back  then right in the 1970s and then my dad  said listen if you win this you win me  this ten bucks  I'm giving you five bucks of that money  and I said oh okay now I've got a real  incentive and I that I turn to my  grandpa and I said what about you what  if I lose and he said you're not getting  anything for me I'm keeping the whole  ten bucks and so now I have like a  double incentive actually triple  incentive I love to eat and I wanted to  like you know help my dad out and I was  gonna get five bucks out of it which  could go quite a long way I mean back  then a candy bar was a quarter so you  know think about how much candy that  could could buy so they bring it out and  it's on this giant plate right so I  started you know cutting into the steak  and it's good you know and I start  eating the lobster tail and that's good  too you know and I'm filling up and  filling up and I more or less like wolf  the whole steak down and probably in  retrospect what I should have done is  eat the lobster first and then eat the  steak after because the lobsters richer  and you know I get I get all of that  then and then there's probably like a  third of the lobster tail left on the  plate and I'm feeling really like  stuffed like full you know like to the  point where I don't know if I can eat  anymore  and my grandpa's looking he's like see I  told you he's not gonna be able to eat  that and that kind of put me over the  edge I was you know I won't say I said  like screw you or anything like that I  was I was not that sort of kid back then  I would become so later on but I was  like well who are you to tell me I can't  eat all this food and so you know I'm  still  there and it was I'm not gonna say it's  like a marathon or something silly thing  like that but it was an effort to you  know eat this stuff I kind of felt like  I might you know throw up or something  but I'm forcing the food down and my dad  and my grandpa and everybody else around  the table too is watching and then  people at the other tables are all kind  of in on it as well because they've  heard my dad and grandpa talking and my  mom is getting a little bit you know  sort of alternately embarrassed and also  enjoying the attention I don't know what  my sister was doing because I was  concentrating on that plate I was you  know single-minded I like I'm not gonna  throw this up because I don't lose the  bat you know I'm gonna get this food  down and then I'm gonna get my my five  dollars in my moment of glory and I did  it you know I got it down and I won't  say that I actually enjoyed the last bit  of that meal the way I did the front end  of that meal but I managed to do it and  then you know my grandpa paid over the  ten bucks and my dad gave me the five  bucks right there and then we you know  eventually they had dessert I didn't  have any dessert  of course that's just sitting there and  you know just stuffed to the gills as we  say and then we went home and I had kind  of a stomachache but I had five dollars  more I didn't have a wallet back then  and five dollars more in my pocket and I  knew that I could do it and that I liked  steak and lobster together so you know I  kind of learned some sort of less than  in the process and so did my grandpa  don't bet against this kid eating stuff  so there's my story not a particularly  edifying one but perhaps an interesting  and enjoyable one  you
 

